%[text] %[text:anchor:T_BOOSTPFC] # Design and Analyze a Boost PFC Converter
%[text] A boost power factor correction (PFC) converter converts single-phase AC to a regulated DC bus voltage and draws a sinusoidal line current at unity power factor. This design solution shows how to size the power stage, select the semiconductor devices, and analyze the waveforms, harmonics, power loss and efficiency. The model uses the boost PFC plant and controller blocks from `ConverterLibrary`.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_scope] ## Design Solution Scope
%[text] In this design solution, you:
%[text] - Size the power stage and review the estimated design parameters
%[text] - Select boost-leg and rectifier-bridge semiconductors that meet the required ratings
%[text] - Simulate the closed loop and inspect the AC input and DC output waveforms
%[text] - Analyze the switching ripple, the line-frequency harmonics and the total harmonic distortion (THD)
%[text] - Estimate power loss and efficiency \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_sys] ## System Overview
%[text] The model contains the boost PFC plant and controller. A diode bridge rectifies the line voltage, so one boost leg serves both half cycles with two diode forward drops in the conduction path.
%[text] The controller regulates the DC bus with an outer voltage loop that outputs a power command. An inner current loop shapes the boost-inductor current into a rectified sine wave locked to the line, which gives unity power factor.![](text:image:2fed)
modelName = "BoostPFCConverter";
open_system(modelName);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_design] ## Select Boost PFC Converter Parameters
%[text] To size the power stage, use the `EstimateBoostPFCParameters` live script. From your design requirements, it estimates the boost inductance, the DC bus capacitance and the device ratings.
%[text] To open the parameter estimation workflow, enter this command in the MATLAB® Command Window:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'EstimateBoostPFCParameters'
%[text] ```
%[text] Load the default parameters.
if ~exist('boostPFCDesign','var')
    BoostPFCDefaultInputParameters;
end
disp(table(designTable,'VariableNames',"Boost PFC Estimated Design Parameters")); %[output:88a92ed2]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_selectcomp] ## Select Converter Components
%[text] To select the ratings of the boost-leg and rectifier-bridge semiconductors and catalog parts that meet them, use the `SelectBoostPFCComponents` live script. The parts come from the Simscape™ parts feature.
%[text] To open the component selection workflow, enter this command in the MATLAB Command Window:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'SelectBoostPFCComponents'
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_op] ## Define the Operating Point
%[text] Set the DC bus voltage and the load power for the waveform, harmonic and loss analyses.
%[text] The harmonic analysis reuses the waveform run, which logs finely enough to resolve the switching ripple.
voltageReference = simscape.Value(boostPFCDesign.outputVoltage.value, "V");  % DC bus setpoint (Vref)
outputLoadPower  = simscape.Value(boostPFCDesign.ratedPower.value, "W");     % Output power (Pload)
rippleSpanMultiple = 10;                                                     % Ripple spectrum span, as a multiple of the switching frequency
maximumSolverStep  = 1/(2*rippleSpanMultiple*switchingFrequency.value);       % Maximum solver step (s) that resolves the ripple span
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_plot] ### Plot Simulation Results
%[text] To plot the AC input voltage and current, the boost-inductor current and the DC bus voltage, use `getConverterWaveforms`. It simulates the fast `Simplified` variant with ideal devices.
%[text] To set how many line cycles at the end of the simulation are plotted, use `FocusCycles`.
simulationTime   = simscape.Value(150e-3, "s");  % Stop time of the waveform run

[boostPFCIVData, boostPFCSimulationLog] = getConverterWaveforms(modelName, Converter="BoostPFC", ... %[output:group:b3caea79] %[output:b2cae8e6]
    Vref=voltageReference, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:b2cae8e6]
    SimTime=simulationTime, FrequencyHz=lineFrequency.value, FocusCycles=2, ... %[output:b2cae8e6]
    MaxStep=maximumSolverStep); %[output:group:b3caea79] %[output:b2cae8e6]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_harm] ### Harmonic Analysis and THD
%[text] To analyze the input AC current, use `estimateConverterHarmonics`. It reads the log of the waveform run and plots:
%[text] - The switching-ripple spectrum up to `RippleSpanMultiple` times the switching frequency
%[text] - The line-frequency harmonics up to the 10th, with the THD in the title, and the THD by standard (IEEE 519 and IEC 61000) \
harmonicData = estimateConverterHarmonics(boostPFCSimulationLog, Converter="BoostPFC", ... %[output:group:3814f4ea] %[output:8a73c0e9] %[output:87348c68] %[output:41b2b1ce]
    FundamentalHz=lineFrequency.value, SwitchingHz=switchingFrequency.value, ... %[output:8a73c0e9] %[output:87348c68] %[output:41b2b1ce]
    RippleSpanMultiple=rippleSpanMultiple, ... %[output:8a73c0e9] %[output:87348c68] %[output:41b2b1ce]
    ShowHarmonicTable=false); %[output:group:3814f4ea] %[output:8a73c0e9] %[output:87348c68] %[output:41b2b1ce]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_loss] ### Estimate Power Loss and Efficiency
%[text] To estimate the switching and conduction losses and the efficiency, use `getConverterPowerLoss`. It simulates the plant's `Detailed` variant.
%[text] The loss is averaged over the last `MinCycles` line cycles of the run. Set `simulationTime` so that the converter reaches steady state before them.
simulationTime   = simscape.Value(60e-3, "s");  % Stop time of the loss run, three line cycles at 50 Hz

lossData = getConverterPowerLoss(modelName, Converter="BoostPFC", ... %[output:group:687b5dcb] %[output:7e3839d3] %[output:1b6297d7]
    Vref=voltageReference, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:7e3839d3] %[output:1b6297d7]
    SimTime=simulationTime, SwitchingLossDetail="lumped", ... %[output:7e3839d3] %[output:1b6297d7]
    FrequencyHz=lineFrequency.value, MinCycles=1, MaxStep=1e-5); %[output:group:687b5dcb] %[output:7e3839d3] %[output:1b6297d7]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_concl] ## Conclusion
%[text] This design solution shows how to use MATLAB scripting and Simscape Electrical™ to design a boost PFC converter. It sizes the power stage and selects the semiconductors, models the circuit at low fidelity for fast simulation, harmonic analysis and control design, and models it in enough detail to predict losses and efficiency.
